[MEDIUM] stop.sh blindly trusts PID file without checking process identity
  Recipe:   Start any unrelated long-lived process. Note its PID. Write that PID into server.pid. Run ./stop.sh. The script kills the unrelated process and removes the PID file.
  Evidence: stop.sh only checks 'kill -0 $PID' (process exists), never verifies the PID actually belongs to a python running server.py. PID reuse after reboot is the common case (Linux reuses PIDs).
